    "narrative": "The marquee play for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is undoubtedly **James Wood**, who commands a 74.53 score against the right-handed G. Holmes. **James Wood**\u2019s elite .423 xwOBA and the substantial 17% SP Skill boost make him our primary offensive anchor, and while the opposing pitcher's hold rate creates a marginal headwind for stolen bases, **James Wood**\u2019s position at the top of the order remains the most valuable real estate in our lineup. **Tyler Soderstrom** also presents a high-upside play at 69.03, buoyed by a significant 10% park factor bump that helps him navigate the moderate threat posed by Sonny Gray. While **Josh Naylor** faces a more formidable arm in Sasaki, his 64.16 score reflects his reliability as a middle-of-the-order producer who remains resistant to platoon-based volatility.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the card involved **Dalton Rushing**, whose 44.02 score places him on the fringe of our starting threshold. While his raw metrics were buoyed by a favorable platoon split, the combination of a bottom-of-the-order assignment and a sub-optimal matchup against Woo keeps his ceiling contained. Conversely, **Cole Young**\u2019s 52.39 score narrowly edges out the competition for the 2B slot, primarily due to the 10% boost afforded by his leadoff role and the situational synergy of a slow opposing delivery that keeps our pursuit of stolen bases alive. We are managing the bench carefully, as the absences of **Corbin Carroll** and **Mickey Moniak** due to schedule gaps, coupled with the ongoing IL stint for **Vinnie Pasquantino**, have severely limited our flexibility. With **Jose Caballero** currently benched due to a poor matchup against S. Burke and a lack of confirmed lineup presence, we are relying heavily on our current starters to maintain efficiency across our limited daily game caps.",
